<?xpacket end="w"?>
endstreamendobj2203 0 obj<</A 2204 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 who have been in the Scheme for three years, the percentage of families/carers working in a paid job was 44.9% at baseline, 49.2% at first review, 51.4% at second review and 53.0% at third review. The\ Australian population benchmark was 73.9%.\r\nOf those with a paid job, the percentage who work 15 hours or more per week was 79.0% at baseline, 82.0% at first review, 83.6% at second review and 85.2% at third review.\r\nOf those with a paid job, the per\centage who work 30 hours or more per week was 39.1% at baseline, 42.7% at first review, 45.5% at second review and 48.9% at third review. The Australian population benchmark was 61.9%.\r\nThe percentage of families/carers and their partners who are able \to work as much as they want decreases from 39.5% at baseline to 39.9% at first review, to 38.3% at second review and 37.7% at third review.\r\nOf families/carers and their partners who are unable to work as much as they want, the percentage who say that \the situation of their child/family member with disability is a barrier to working more increases from 90.3 % at baseline to 92.4% at first review, to 93.8% at second review and 94.9% at third review.\r\nOf families/carers and their partners who are unabl\e to work as much as they want, the percentage who say that the availability of jobs is a barrier to working more increases from 18.4% at baseline to 21.3% at first review, to 21.9% at second review and 23.9% at third review.\r\nOf families/carers and the\ir partners who are unable to work as much as they want, the percentage who say that insufficient flexibility of jobs is a barrier to working more increases from 39.7% at baseline to 45.1% at first review, to 47.9% at second review and then slightly decre\ases to 47.8% at third review.\r\n␀)/K[2207 0 R 89]/P 2200 0 R/Pg 118 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2212 0 obj<</A 2213 0 R/Alt(For mothers of participants aged 0 to 14 who have been in the Scheme for three years, the percentage working in a paid job was 43.8% at baseline, 48.2% at first review, 50.9% at second review and 52.2% at third review. The Australian population benchmark \was 73.2%.\r\nFor fathers of participants, the percentage working in a paid job was 61.2% at baseline, 63.1% at first review, 60.2% at second review and 65.5% at third review. The Australian population benchmark was 84.6%.\r\n␀)/K 90/P 2200 0 R/Pg 118 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2216 0 obj<</A 2217 0 R/Alt(For mothers of participants who are working in a paid job, the percentage who work 30 hours or more per week was 34.7% at baseline, 38.6% at first review, 42.4% at second review and 45.1% at third review. The Australian population benchmark was 60.2%.\r\n\For fathers of participants who are working in a paid job, the percentage who work 30 hours or more per week was 82.2% at baseline, 84.7% at first review, 84.2% at second review and 86.8% at third review. The Australian population benchmark was 88.6%.\r\n\␀)/K 91/P 2200 0 R/Pg 118 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2223 0 obj<</A 2224 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 who have been in the Scheme for two years, the percentage of families/carers working in a paid job was 47.4% at baseline, 50.4% at first review, and 51.1% at second review. The Australian population b\enchmark was 73.9%.\r\nOf those with a paid job, the percentage who work 15 hours or more per week was 77.4% at baseline, 80.6% at first review and 83.0% at second review.\r\nOf those with a paid job, the percentage who work 30 hours or more per week was \40.6% at baseline, 44.3% at first review and 47.1% at second review. The Australian population benchmark was 61.9%.\r\nThe percentage of families/carers and their partners who are able to work as much as they want was 38.5% at baseline, 38.3% at first rev\iew and 38.4% at second review.\r\nOf families/carers and their partners who are unable to work as much as they want, the percentage who say that the situation of their child/family member with disability was a barrier to working more increases from 88.5%\ at baseline to 91.4% at first review and 92.9% at second review.\r\nOf families/carers and their partners who are unable to work as much as they want, the percentage who say that the availability of jobs was a barrier to working more increases from 19.0%\ at baseline to 21.4% at first review and to 23.1% at second review.\r\nOf families/carers and their partners who are unable to work as much as they want, the percentage who say that insufficient flexibility of jobs was a barrier to working more increases\ from 39.8% at baseline to 44.1% at first review, and to 46.0% at second review.\r\n␀)/K[2227 0 R 34]/P 2222 0 R/Pg 125 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2232 0 obj<</A 2233 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 who have been in the Scheme for one year, the percentage of families/carers working in a paid job has increased from 47.9% at baseline to 49.7% at first review. The Australian population benchmark was\ 73.9%.\r\nOf those with a paid job, the percentage who work 15 hours or more per week was 79.2% at baseline, and 81.2% at first review.\r\nOf those with a paid job, the percentage who work 30 hours or more per week was 42.2% at baseline and 44.5% at firs\t review. The Australian population benchmark was 61.9%.\r\nThe percentage of families/carers and their partners who are able to work as much as they want was 39.9% at baseline and 39.8% at first review.\r\nOf families/carers and their partners who are un\able to work as much as they want, the percentage who say that the situation of their child/family member with disability was a barrier to working more increases from 88.8% at baseline to 90.8% at first review.\r\nOf families/carers and their partners who\ are unable to work as much as they want, the percentage who say that the availability of jobs was a barrier to working more increases from 16.6% at baseline to 18.6% at first review.\r\nOf families/carers and their partners who are unable to work as much\ as they want, the percentage who say that insufficient flexibility of jobs was a barrier to working more increases from 36.1% at baseline to 39.4% at first review.␀)/K[2236 0 R 35]/P 2222 0 R/Pg 125 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2288 0 obj<</A 2289 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 who have been in the Scheme for three years, the percentage of families/carers who know what specialist services are needed to promote their childs learning and development was 41.4% at baseline, 50.\0% at first review, 53.3% at second review and 53.3% at third review. \r\nThe percentage of respondents who know what they can do to support their childs learning and development was 42.5% at baseline, 50.4% at first review, 52.8% at second review and 51\.9% at third review. \r\nThe percentage who get enough support in parenting their child was 44.2% at baseline, 48.9% at first review, 49.3% at second review, and 48.4% at third review.\r\nThe percentage who say that their relationship with services was go\od or very good was 85.5% at baseline, 87.2% at first review, 89.7% at second review and 90.6% at third review.\r\n␀)/K[2292 0 R 26]/P 2287 0 R/Pg 132 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2293 0 obj<</A 2294 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 who have been in the Scheme for two years, the percentage of families/carers who know what specialist services are needed to promote their childs learning and development was 41.0% at baseline, 50.0%\ at first review, and 53.3% at second review. \r\nThe percentage of respondents who know what they can do to support their childs learning and development was 42.1% at baseline, 49.4% at first review and 52.1% at second review. \r\nThe percentage who get\ enough support in parenting their child was 44.2% at baseline, 47.9% at first review and 49.2% at second review.\r\nThe percentage who say that their relationship with services was good or very good was 76.3% at baseline, 87.4% at first review and 89.5% \at second review.\r\n␀)/K[2297 0 R 27]/P 2287 0 R/Pg 132 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2299 0 obj<</A 2300 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 who have been in the Scheme for one year, the percentage of families/carers who know what specialist services are needed to promote their childs learning and development was 41.2% at baseline and 49.\5% at first review. \r\nThe percentage of respondents who know what they can do to support their childs learning and development was 41.6% at baseline and 48.6% at first review. \r\nThe percentage who get enough support in parenting their child was 44.2%\ at baseline and 48.2% at first review.\r\nThe percentage who say that their relationship with services was good or very good was 80.6% at baseline and 88.1 % at first review.\r\n␀)/K[2303 0 R 32]/P 2298 0 R/Pg 140 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2353 0 obj<</A 2354 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 who have been in the Scheme for three years, the percentage who have friends they can see as often as they like has decreased from 45.0% at baseline to 44.0% at first review, to 41.3% at second review\ and 38.6% at third review. \r\nThe percentage of respondents who have people they can ask for practical help as often as they need has decreased from 41.6% at baseline, to 41.0% at first review, to 38.0% at second review and 34.9% at third review. \r\nTh\e percentage who have people they can ask for childcare as often as they need has decreased from 29.5% at baseline to 27.5% at first review, to 25.5% at second review and 23.9% at third review.\r\nThe percentage who have someone they can talk to for emoti\onal support as often as they need was 60.8% at baseline, 63.1% at first review, 63.6% at second review and 62.5% at third review.\r\nThe percentage of families or carers and their partners who are able to engage in social interactions and community life \as much as they want was 27.6% at baseline, 26.5% at first review, 23.9% at second review and 23.1% at third review.\r\nThe percentage of families or carers who rate their health as excellent, very good or good has decreased from 74.5% at baseline to 70.6\% at first review, to 67.4% at second review and 64.7% at third review.\r\n␀)/K[2357 0 R 63]/P 2352 0 R/Pg 142 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2479 0 obj<</A 2480 0 R/Alt(For families and carers of participants aged 15 to 24 who have been in the Scheme for three years, the percentage in a paid job was 49.4% at baseline, 54.2% at first review, 56.1% at second review, and 54.4% at third review. \r\nOf those with a paid job, \the percentage working 15 hours or more per week was 85.1% at baseline, 87.0% at first review, 88.0% at second review, and 90.2% at third review.\r\nOf those with a paid job, the percentage working 30 hours or more per week was 52.9% at baseline, 59.6% at\ first review, 57.7% at second review, and 60.0% at third review.\r\n␀)/K[2483 0 R 33]/P 2459 0 R/Pg 155 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2485 0 obj<</A 2486 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 15 to 24 who have been in the Scheme for two years, the percentage working in a paid job was 51.2% at baseline, 55.1% at first review, and 53.4% at second review. \r\nOf those with a paid job, the percentage \who work 15 hours or more per week was 84.1% at baseline, 85.9% at first review and 86.4% at second review.\r\nOf those with a paid job, the percentage who work 30 hours or more per week was 49.0% at baseline, 51.9% at first review and 54.2% at second rev\iew. \r\nThe percentage of families or carers who provide informal care to the family member with disability and are able to work as much as they want was 44.3% at baseline, 44.5% at first review and 44.9% at second review.\r\nOf those who are unable to w\ork as much as they want, the percentage who say that the availability of jobs was a barrier to working more was 16.0% at baseline, 19.1% at first review and 19.7% at second review.\r\n␀)/K[2489 0 R 32]/P 2484 0 R/Pg 161 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2490 0 obj<</A 2491 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 15 to 24 who have been in the Scheme for one year, the percentage of families/carers working in a paid job was 51.4% at baseline and 52.7% at first review. \r\nOf those with a paid job, the percentage who wor\k 15 hours or more per week was 85.2% at baseline, and 86.7% at first review.\r\nOf those with a paid job, the percentage who work 30 hours or more per week was 53.1% at baseline and 55.1% at first review. \r\nThe percentage of families or carers who prov\ide informal care to the family member with disability and are able to work as much as they want was 45.1% at baseline and 46.1% at first review.\r\nOf those who are unable to work as much as they want, the percentage who say that the availability of jobs\ was a barrier to working more was 14.9% at baseline and 17.1% at the first review.\r\n␀)/K[2494 0 R 33]/P 2484 0 R/Pg 161 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2529 0 obj<</A 2530 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 15 to 24 who have been in the Scheme for three years, the percentage who say the services their family member with disability and their family receive meet their needs was 17.7% at baseline, 31.5% at first re\view, 34.3% at second review and 36.3% at third review. \r\nThe percentage of respondents who say that the services they use listen to them was 64.0% at baseline, 70.3% at first review, 73.3% at second review and 72.0% at third review. \r\nThe percentage \who say the services their family member with disability and their family receive help them plan for the future was 67.4% at baseline, 62.8% at first review, 62.8% at second review and 72.1% at third review.\r\nThe percentage who strongly agree or agree t\hat they feel confident about the future of their family member was 50.0% at baseline, 70.5% at first review, 63.6% at second review then 75.0% at third review.\r\nThe percentage who strongly agree or agree that their family member gets the support they n\eed was 40.9% at baseline, 45.5% at first review, 43.2% at second review then 70.5% at third review.\r\n␀)/K[2533 0 R 32]/P 2528 0 R/Pg 168 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2534 0 obj<</A 2535 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 15 to 24 who have been in the Scheme for two years, the percentage of families/carers who say the services their family member with disability and their family receive meet their needs has increased from 17.8\% at baseline to 28.5% at first review and then to 30.5% at second review. \r\nThe percentage of respondents who say that the services they use listen to them has increased from 62.2% at baseline, to 67.7% at first review and then 71.1% at second review. \\r\nThe percentage who say the service their family member with disability and their family receive help them plan for the future was 58.5% at baseline, 75.8% at first review and then 78.0% at second review.\r\nThe percentage who strongly agree or agree t\hat they feel confident about the future of their family member was 50.0% at baseline, 69.8% at first review and then 68.6% at second review.\r\nThe percentage who strongly agree or agree that their family member gets the support they need was 30.2% at ba\seline, 57.0% at first review and then 58.3% at second.\r\n␀)/K[2538 0 R 33]/P 2528 0 R/Pg 168 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2541 0 obj<</A 2542 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 15 to 24 who have been in the Scheme for one year, the percentage of families/carers who say the services their family member with disability and their family receive meet their needs has increased from 17.8%\ at baseline to 24.9% at first review. \r\nThe percentage of respondents who say that the services they use listen to them has increased from 65.5% at baseline to 69.7% at first review. \r\nThe percentage who say the service their family member with disab\ility and their family receive help them plan for the future was 57.4% at baseline and 71.9% at first review.\r\nThe percentage who strongly agree or agree that they feel confident about the future of their family member was 56.1% at baseline and 64.4% at\ first review.\r\nThe percentage who strongly agree or agree that their family member gets the support they need was 32.6% at baseline, and 53.0% at first review.\r\n␀)/K[2545 0 R 22]/P 2540 0 R/Pg 176 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2570 0 obj<</A 2571 0 R/Alt(For families and carers of participants aged 15 to 24 who have been in the Scheme for three years, the percentage rating their health as excellent, very good or good was 64.9% at baseline, 61.3% at first review, 55.5% at second review and 56.6% at third r\eview.\r\nFor families and carers of participants aged 15 to 24 who have been in the Scheme for two years, the percentage rating their health as excellent, very good or good was 60.9% at baseline, 59.0% at first review, and 55.1% at second review.\r\nFor \families and carers of participants aged 15 to 24 who have been in the Scheme for one year, the percentage rating their health as excellent, very good or good was 61.1% at baseline, and 58.4% at first review.\r\n␀)/K[2573 0 R 35]/P 2569 0 R/Pg 178 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj2757 0 obj<</A 2758 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 25 and over who have been in the Scheme for three years, the percentage of families/carers who are in a paid job was 37.5% at baseline, 34.0% at review 1, 40.7% at review 2 and 35.0% at review 3.\r\nThe perce\ntage of respondents who say the services their family member with disability and their family receive meet their needs was 23.8% at baseline, 40.4% at review 1, 44.4% at review 2 and 36.3% at review 3.\r\nThe percentage of respondents who say the service\s helped them to plan for the future was 40.9% at baseline, 50.0% at review 1, 72.7% at review 2 and 72.7% at review 3.\r\nThe percentage of respondents who strongly agree or agree that services and supports have helped them to better care for their famil\y member with disability was 36.4% at baseline, 54.5% at review 1, 59.1% at review 2 and 77.3% at review 3.\r\n␀)/K[2761 0 R 32]/P 2756 0 R/Pg 193 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2762 0 obj<</A 2763 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 25 and over who have been in the Scheme for two years, the percentage of families/carers who are in a paid job was 33.5% at baseline, 30.9% at review 1 and 31.9% at review 2.\r\nThe percentage of respondents \who say the services their family member with disability and their family receive meet their needs was 21.3% at baseline, 31.6% at review 1 and 33.6% at review 2.\r\nThe percentage of respondents who say the services helped them to plan for the future was\ 63.6% at baseline, 79.0% at review 1 and 74.7% at review 2.\r\nThe percentage of respondents who strongly agree or agree that services and supports have helped them to better care for their family member with disability was 54.6% at baseline, 66.9% at re\view 1 and 73.6% at review 2.\r\n␀)/K[2766 0 R 33]/P 2756 0 R/Pg 193 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj2768 0 obj<</A 2769 0 R/Alt(Among families and carers of participants aged 25 and over who have been in the Scheme for one year, the percentage of families/carers who are in a paid job was 34.7% at baseline and 34.0% at review 1.\r\nThe percentage of respondents who say the services\ their family member with disability and their family meet receive their needs was 20.0% at baseline and 27.0% at review 1.\r\nThe percentage of respondents who say the services they use listen to them was 68.5% at baseline and 71.8% at review 1.\r\nThe p\ercentage of respondents who say the services helped them to plan for the future was 66.5% at baseline and 73.8% at review 1.\r\nThe percentage of respondents who strongly agree or agree that services and supports have helped them to better care for their\ family member with disability was 48.1% at baseline and 69.9% at review 1.\r\nThe percentage of respondents who strongly agree or agree that they feel more confident about the future of their family with disability under the NDIS was 48.1% at baseline an\d 66.7% at review 1.\r\nThe percentage who feel delighted, pleased or mostly satisfied when thinking about last year and what they expect for the future was 47.6% at baseline and 58.7% at review 1.\r\n␀)/K[2772 0 R 28]/P 2767 0 R/Pg 201 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj28627 0 obj<</A 28628 0 R/Alt(This chart shows the relationship between answers to Has the NDIS helped? questions and other outcomes framework questions. The general trend was that, families and carers who answer positively to the five questions detailed above are more likely to ans\wer positively to the Has NDIS helped question?Ž compared to the population average. The difference to the population average ranges from approximately -25% to +10%.␀)/ID 28629 0 R/K[28630 0 R 2]/P 28621 0 R/Pg 620 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj28637 0 obj<</A 28638 0 R/Alt(This chart shows the relationship between answers to Satisfaction with the amount of sayŽ questions and other outcomes framework questions. The general trend was that, families and carers who answer positively to the five questions detailed above are mor\e likely to answer positively to the Satisfaction with the amount of sayŽ questions compared to the population average. The difference to the population average ranges from approximately -20% to +10%.␀)/ID 28639 0 R/K[28640 0 R 1]/P 28635 0 R/Pg 627 0 R/S/Figure>>endobjxref
